    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\n\n- [X] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [X] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\n\n### Module(s)\n\nmini.ai\n\n### Description\n\nWith the default config, typing `vinf` will select inside the call signature of the next function call. And typing `inf` again will select inside the next call signature, and so on. Unlike `c` and `d` operations, the dot repeat for this doesn't work because vim doesn't have a native mechanism for \"remembering\" the last text-object operation.\r\n\r\nYet imo, this feature would be very usefu, and would be analogous to `fa;;;` for jumping to successive `a` chars within a line.\r\n\r\nDoes this feature exist, or can you think of a way to hack it?",
