        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> Thank you for clarifying. If the aim is to adhere to neovim built-in definitions shouldn't `;` then work?\r\n> \r\n> So that : `vaf` + `af` could also be executed as: `vaf` + `;`\r\n\r\nI think exactly the opposite, as `;` is documented as \"Repeat latest f, t, F or T [count] times.\". And even if it was \"repeat latest motion...\", it would still be not a good candidate, because `af` is not a motion (i.e. move cursor from current position), but textobject (i.e. select specific region less dependency on the exact cursor position).",
            "created_at": "2024-07-23T05:47:46Z",
            "html_url": "https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/issues/521#issuecomment-2244303499",
